<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-s10e02" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Episode 2: Solve Systems of Equations by Substitution</title>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 2 Learning Goals</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Solve systems by substitution
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 2 Key Terminology</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Substitution
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <p>
    If we have a formula involving <m>y</m>, and if we know that <m>y=4</m>, we can replace <m>y</m> with 4 throughout the formula:
    <md>
      <mrow>3x+2y \amp =1</mrow>
      <mrow>3x+2(4) \amp =1</mrow>
    </md>
    If we have a formula a formula involving <m>y</m>, and if we know that <m>y=2x+5</m>, we can replace <m>y</m> with <m>2x+5</m> throughout the formula:
    <md>
      <mrow>3x+2y \amp =1</mrow>
      <mrow>3x+2(2x+5) \amp =1</mrow>
    </md>
  </p>
  <definition xml:id="def-substitution">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Replacing a variable with an equal expression is called <term>substitution</term>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    <alert>Solving Systems using Substitution:</alert>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          	Solve the first equation for <m>y</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          	Substitute the first equation into the second equation. This equation should now contain only <m>x</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          	Solve the second equation for <m>x</m>. 
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          	Plug this value of <m>x</m> back into the equation you got at the end of step 1 to find <m>y</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      Solve the system of equations:
      <md>
        \begin{cases} x+ y \amp = 1\\ 2x-y \amp = 4 \end{cases}
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: Solve the first equation for <m>y</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow>x+y \amp =1</mrow>
        <mrow>x+y-x \amp =1-x</mrow>
        <mrow>y \amp =1-x</mrow>
      </md>
      Substitute this into the second equation:
      <md>
        <mrow>2x-y \amp =4 </mrow>
        <mrow>2x-(1-x) \amp =4 </mrow>
        <mrow>2x-1+x \amp =4 </mrow>
        <mrow>3x-1 \amp =4 </mrow>
        <mrow>3x \amp =5 </mrow>
        <mrow>x \amp =\frac{5}{3} </mrow>
      </md>
      Now plug this value of <m>x</m> back into the equation <m>y=1-x</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow>y= \amp 1-\frac{5}{3}</mrow>
        <mrow>y= \amp \frac{3}{3}-\frac{5}{3}</mrow>
        <mrow>y= \amp -\frac{2}{3}</mrow>
      </md>
      So, the solution to the system is:
      <md>
        (x,y) = \left(\frac{5}{3},-\frac{2}{3}\right)
      </md>
      
    </p>
  </example>
  <worksheet xml:id="ws-s10e02">
    <title>Season 10, Episode 2 Practice Problems</title>
    <p>
      Solve the following systems using substitution.
    </p>
    <exercise workspace="3.0in">
      <p>
        <m>
        \begin{cases} 3x-y \amp = -5\\ y \amp = 2x+4 \end{cases}
      </m>
        </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="3.0in">
      <p>
        <m>
        \begin{cases} 3x-2y \amp = 2\\ y \amp = \frac{1}{2}+3 \end{cases}
      </m>
        </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="3.0in">
      <p>
        <m>
        \begin{cases} x-y \amp = 0\\ 2x+5y \amp = -14 \end{cases}
      </m>
        </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="3.0in">
      <p>
        <m>
        \begin{cases} y \amp = -2x+7\\ y \amp = \frac{2}{3}x-1 \end{cases}
      </m>
        </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="3.0in">
      <p>
        <m>
        \begin{cases} y \amp = -5x\\ 5x+y \amp = 6 \end{cases}
      </m>
        </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="3.0in">
      <p>
        <m>
        \begin{cases} y \amp = -\frac{1}{3}x+2\\ x+3y \amp = 6 \end{cases}
      </m>
        </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="3.0in">
      <p>
        <m>
        \begin{cases} 2x+y \amp = 7\\ x-2y \amp = 6 \end{cases}
      </m>
        </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="3.0in">
      <p>
        <m>
        \begin{cases} x+3y \amp = 10\\ 4x+y \amp = 18 \end{cases}
      </m>
        </p>
    </exercise>
  </worksheet>
</section>  